# This tree is BOTH the submission bundle and the published artifact repository,
# and the two want different contents.
#
# The manuscript belongs on arXiv, which gives it an identifier and a version
# history that a PDF in a git repository does not.  A paper committed here would
# go stale the first time the manuscript is revised, with nothing on its face to
# say which version it was -- and once the paper is accepted, a bare PDF with no
# copyright notice is a needless tangle.  So git tracks the code and the video;
# the paper stays local, for uploading where it belongs.
#
# `code/paper/` IS NOT PUBLISHED AT ALL -- authors' decision, 2026-08-27.  It
# holds the manuscript sources, the figure and video makers, `audit_paper.py`
# and `verify_prop9_proof.py`: the machinery that BUILDS the paper rather than
# the science it reports.  All of it still ships to reviewers inside
# `code.tar.gz`, which is confidential; only the public mirror drops it.
#
# WHAT THAT COSTS, said plainly rather than discovered later: a clone can
# re-solve every experiment and re-read every gate, but it cannot re-draw the
# figures, re-render the videos, or run the audit that ties each number in the
# text back to its artifact.  `run_all.sh` skips those steps when the directory
# is absent instead of failing, and the README says where they live.
#
# `artifact-anonymous.zip` is the bundle for double-anonymous review.  It is a
# copy of what is already in this repository, minus the identity, and a repo
# that carries the authors' names has no use for it.
artifact-anonymous.zip
automatica/
automatica_anon/
extendedfalse_check/
arxiv/
abstract.txt
UPLOADING.md
cover_letter.txt
*-source.zip
*-source.tar.gz
code.tar.gz
code/paper/
